    },
    {
      "modify_from": "With theta the current TCP yaw, command yaw wrap_to_pi(theta + atan2(ny,nx) - atan2(uy,ux)), where wrap_to_pi expresses the angle in [-pi,pi]. This rotates the long free end to point along n while preserving its signed angular attachment to the hand.",
      "modify_to": "With theta the current TCP yaw, command yaw wrap_to_pi(theta + atan2(-ny,-nx) - atan2(uy,ux)), where wrap_to_pi expresses the angle in [-pi,pi]. This rotates the long free end to point along -n while preserving its signed angular attachment to the hand."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "When the held bar is low at this staging position, move straight along d to T-0.03d-0.10n, z=0.02, closed, without changing orientation.",
      "modify_to": "When the held bar is low behind the cube and the closed TCP is within the stated arrival tolerance of the staging position, prioritize the pushing stroke over restaging. Motion frames showing descent into this configuration support recognizing arrival; do not repeat staging or reverse the bar orientation because a fresh localization differs slightly from the reached pose. Move straight along d to T-0.03d+0.10n, z=0.02, closed, preserving the established staging orientation."
    }
  ],
  "refused": "The round-2 commitment selecting the negative lateral TCP offset and a free end pointing along +n is replaced, rather than retained: this round reports that it produces opposite-side staging in both raised-bar observations and prevents progression at subsequent low staging observations. The replacement changes staging, directed yaw, and stroke endpoint consistently. The old negative-sign stroke expression requested in individual low-stage reports is not committed because it would cross laterally from the corrected staging side. Round 1's grasp-point definition and endpoint-opening rule remain unchanged, as do round 2's directed-attachment yaw calculation principle and prohibition on reducing staging yaw modulo pi. No memory changes are needed."
}
